DLS(1)                         ZOAU Command Syntax                        DLS(1)

NAME

	dls - List datasets that match a pattern. These can be non-VSAM datasets,
	generation datasets (GDS), generation data groups (GDG), and VSAM datasets.

SYNOPSIS

	dls [-abBdFhiHjJlmpqsStTuUv] [-V <VOLSER>] [<DATASET_PATTERN>]+

	-a
		List members and member aliases.

	-b
		List dataset block size along with detailed information.

	-B
		List used and maximum directory blocks.

	-d, --debug
		Print out debug messages.

	-F
		Display the catalog entry type identifier.
		Must be used with the -t option.

	-H, --header
		Display a header for extended listings.

	-h, --help
		Display syntax help.

	-i
		List VSAM integrity attributes along with detailed information.

	-j, --json
		Print the output in JSON format, instead of a list.
		JSON output is enabled when detailed information is
		requested with one of the -blsu options.

	-J, --prettyjson
		Prints JSON output in a readable format.
		This option forces -j if it was not set.

	-l
		Display detailed information based on the dataset type.

	--list-volser
		Display the volume serial for non-VSAM datasets.

	-m
		List migrated datasets.

	-p
		List VSAM performance attributes along with detailed information.

	-q, --quiet
		Enable quiet mode, which suppresses all terminal output. The exit value
		is still set.

	-s
		Include the estimated used space (bytes), allocated size (bytes),
		number of allocated extents, first extent size (bytes), and secondary
		space (bytes).

		This information is the space that a dataset occupies in a particular
		volume. A multi-volume dataset will have a different amount of allocated
		space for each of the volumes it resides on.

		These values are estimated using a 3390 device as a reference, and a
		conversion from tracks to bytes.

	-S
		Include the dataset statistics.

	-T
		Include the dataset type.

	-t <TYPE>
		Only display information for the specified catalog entry type.
		TYPE may be one of the following:
		AIX, ALIAS, CLUSTER, GDG, GDS, NONVSAM, PATH, or VSAM

	-u
		List last reference (usage) time along with detailed information.

	-U
		Display dataset sizes in "human-readable" format, such as 1.1K,
		42M, or 123G, where 1K = 1024 bytes.

	-v, --verbose
		Print verbose information.

	-V <VOLSER>
		Only display datasets that are on volume VOLSER.

DESCRIPTION

	dls lists the cataloged datasets that match the specified pattern and writes
	them to stdout.

	Multi-volume data sets produce a line of output for each utilized volume
	when -b, -F, -l, -s, or -u are specified. If the dataset is SMS-managed,
	an asterisk (*) can be present in the volume field instead of a VOLSER. This
	value represents a candidate volume.

	When the -a option is provided, dls prints a list of members and their
	aliases for partitioned non-VSAM datasets. dls lists aliases that are
	equal to or less than eight characters long.

	When the -t option is omitted, dls outputs non-VSAM dataset and generation
	dataset information by default. The supported entry types are:

		* AIX for alternate indexes. (Unrelated to the IBM AIX operating system)
		* ALIAS for non-VSAM dataset aliases.
		* CLUSTER for VSAM clusters and components.
		* GDG for generation data groups.
		* GDS for generation datasets.
		* NONVSAM for non-VSAM datasets.
		* PATH for VSAM paths.
		* VSAM for VSAM clusters including their components.

		When the -t VSAM option is specified, the VSAM components are grouped
		under the name of the cluster, even if the component names do not match
		the provided pattern.

		Use the -t CLUSTER option to list each VSAM entry (base cluster, data
		component or index component) individually. The extended listing options
		are ignored with this filter.

	When you specify the -F option, the catalog type is represented by a single
	character:

		* A for non-VSAM datasets
		* B for generation data groups
		* C for VSAM clusters
		* D for VSAM data components
		* G for VSAM alternate indexes
		* H for generation datasets
		* I for VSAM index components
		* R for VSAM paths
		X for non-VSAM dataset aliases

	When you request detailed information, output is determined by the
	dataset type:

		* For non-VSAM datasets, generation datasets, and dataset aliases:
			* recfm 	Record format
			* lrecl		Logical record length
			* dsorg		Dataset organization
			* volume	Volume serial

		* For generation data groups:
			* limit		GDG limit
			* scratch	SCRATCH | NOSCRATCH attribute
			* empty		EMPTY | NOEMPTY attribute
			* order		LIFO | FIFO attribute
			* purge		PURGE | NOPURGE attribute
			* erase		ERASE | NOERASE attribute

		* For VSAM components (when requested with the -t VSAM option):
			* org 		Data organization
			* klen 		Key length
			* rkp 		Relative key position
			* avglrecl	Average LRECL
			* maxlrecl 	Maximum LRECL
			* bufspace	Buffer space
			* reuse		REUSE | NOREUSE attribute

	The -i option lists the basic VSAM attributes and the following fields:
		* shropts	Share options
		* erase		ERASE | NOERASE attribute

	The -p option lists the basic VSAM attributes and the following fields:
		* cisize	Control interval size
		* spanned	SPANNED | NOSPANNED attribute
		* speed/rec	SPEED | RECOVERY attribute

	The -S option displays dataset statistics. The output is determined by the
	dataset type.

	 For VSAM components (when requested with the -t VSAM option):
		* rec-tot       Total records
		* rec-del       Deleted records
		* rec-ins       Inserted records
		* rec-upd       Updated records
		* rec-ret       Retrieved records
		* splits-ci     Control interval splits
		* splits-ca     Control area splits
		* freespc-%ci   Percentage of space to be left free in CI.
		* freespc-%ca   Percentage of CIs to be left free in a CA.
		* freespc       Bytes of free space in the total allocated amount.

	Specify -m to show migrated datasets. -m cannot be used with options -b,
	-l, -s, or -u.

	When JSON output is requested, the response has the following form:

		{ "datasets": [], "gdgs": [], "vsams": [] }

	The "datasets" array contains non-VSAM and GDS metadata.
	The "gdgs" array contains GDG metadata.
	The "vsams" array contains the metadata of VSAM clusters and components.

DATASET PATTERNS

	The following rules apply to <DATASET_PATTERN>.

	The high-level-qualifier (HLQ) must be specified in full.
	An asterisk (*) matches any sequence of characters.
	A question mark (?) matches a single character.

EXAMPLES

	List all non-VSAM datasets under your default high-level-qualifier:

		dls -l "`hlq`.*"

	Get a JSON object with detailed information for datasets
	under your high-level-qualifier:

		dls -j -bsu "$(hlq).*"

	List all non-VSAM datasets that have an HLQ of TEST followed by TSTR
	and then exactly 3 characters, with a low level qualifier of JCL:

		dls -l 'test.tstr???.*.jcl'

	Test whether a dataset exists:

		dls -q ${prefix}.my.jcl
		if [ $? -eq 0 ]; then
			# dataset match
		fi

	List all the non-VSAM datasets that match the pattern TEST.**.X*
	and volume VOLSER:

		dls -l -V VOLSER TEST.**.X*

EXIT VALUES

	0
		At least one dataset was found matching a <dataset-pattern>

	1
		No datasets were found.

	other
  		Error in dataset listing. See error messages for details.

